From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kiritantakechi <kiritantakechi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 08:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] rockchip: set a NIC's IRQ affinity only when that NIC appears

40-net-smp-affinity ran its whole body on every net "add" event, for
whatever device appeared: each bridge, veth or tunnel set every NIC's
affinity again, and waited up to ten seconds per NIC for its IRQ to
show up in /proc/interrupts. As procd runs hotplug scripts one after
another, a NIC whose IRQ carries another name held up every other net
event behind it for that long.

Set a NIC's affinity when that NIC itself is added. At boot, each NIC's
own event still sets it, so the result is the same.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 .../armv8/base-files/etc/hotplug.d/net/40-net-smp-affinity    | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/target/linux/rockchip/armv8/base-files/etc/hotplug.d/net/40-net-smp-affinity b/target/linux/rockchip/armv8/base-files/etc/hotplug.d/net/40-net-smp-affinity
index 4d734e5..f48b98b 100644
--- a/target/linux/rockchip/armv8/base-files/etc/hotplug.d/net/40-net-smp-affinity
+++ b/target/linux/rockchip/armv8/base-files/etc/hotplug.d/net/40-net-smp-affinity
@@ -23,6 +23,10 @@ set_interface_core() {
 
 	[ -z "${device}" ] && device="$interface"
 
+	# Only when this interface itself appears: any other net device (a
+	# bridge, a veth, a tunnel) would otherwise wait for its IRQ too.
+	[ "${INTERFACE}" = "${interface}" ] || return 0
+
 	local irq=$(get_device_irq "$device")
 
 	echo "${core_mask}" > /proc/irq/${irq}/smp_affinity
